 "issue": 1439,
 "issue_title": "Bug: validate read_jsonl encoding before opening files",
 "issue_body": "## Summary\n`read_jsonl()` documents `encoding` as a string, but it does not validate the option before passing it to `open()`. Invalid values therefore produce raw Python `open()` errors, and `encoding=None` is accepted even though the API contract presents a string-only parameter.\n\n## Why it matters\nCSV readers in Arnio validate encoding-related options before opening files, which gives users consistent and actionable errors. JSONL should follow the same public-API standard, especially because encoding bugs are common when ingesting real-world data files.\n\n## Evidence\n- `arnio/io.py`: `read_jsonl()` accepts `encoding: str = \"utf-8\"` but immediately calls `open(path, encoding=encoding)` without checking the value.\n- `arnio/io.py`: CSV helpers validate encoding options and normalize related errors into Arnio-style messages.\n- `tests/test_jsonl.py` covers valid UTF-8/Latin-1 and decode failures, but does not cover invalid `encoding` types.\n\n## Reproduction or trigger\n1. Create a small `.jsonl` file.\n2. Call `ar.read_jsonl(path, encoding=123)`.\n3. Call `ar.read_jsonl(path, encoding=None)`.\n\n## Expected behavior\n`read_jsonl()` should validate `encoding` before opening the file and raise a clear Arnio-facing error, for example:\n\n- `TypeError: encoding must be a string`\n- `ValueError: Unknown encoding: ...` for unknown codec names\n\n## Actual behavior\nInvalid values are passed to Python `open()` directly. For example, `encoding=123` raises a raw `TypeError` from `open()`, and `encoding=None` is accepted even though the public type contract says `str`.\n\n## Suggested direction\nAdd a small encoding validation block to `read_jsonl()` similar to the CSV path:\n\n1. Reject non-string `encoding` values with `TypeError`.\n2. Optionally validate codec names with `codecs.lookup()` or catch `LookupError` and raise `ValueError` with a clear message.\n3. Add tests for non-string encoding, `None`, unknown encoding names, and the existing valid UTF-8/Latin-1 cases.\n\n## References\n- `arnio/io.py`\n- `tests/test_jsonl.py`\r\n",
 "issue_url": "https://github.com/im-anishraj/arnio/issues/1439",
 "pr": 1544,
 "pr_title": "fix: add encoding validation to read_jsonl()",
 "pr_body": "## Summary\r\nCloses #1439\r\n\r\n`read_jsonl()` was passing `encoding` directly to `open()` without validation, producing raw Python errors instead of clear Arnio-style messages.\r\n\r\n## Changes\r\n- Added `import codecs` to `arnio/io.py`\r\n- Added validation block in `read_jsonl()` before file is opened:\r\n  - Raises `TypeError` for non-string encoding (including `None`)\r\n  - Raises `ValueError` for unknown codec names via `codecs.lookup()`\r\n- Added 3 tests in `tests/test_jsonl.py` covering invalid type, `None`, and unknown codec\r\n\r\n## Testing\r\n- All 37 `test_jsonl.py` tests pass\r\n- Full suite: 1943 passed, 0 failed",
